# .trivyignore.noruby — the suppression surface for the RUBY-FREE operator image.
#
# DELIBERATELY EMPTY, and it must stay that way.
#
# Why it exists as a separate file at all: both image variants used to pass
# `.trivyignore`, which carries 65 waived CVE/GHSA ids. A shared suppression file
# is inherited WHOLE — so the Ruby-free image was scanned with 65 findings waived
# and published as "zero-tolerance UNKNOWN" clean. That is not a measurement of
# the Ruby-free image; it is a measurement of the embedded image's debt applied
# to a different artifact.
#
# The two images do not share a closure. The Ruby-free variant is musl-static on
# distroless-static with no libruby, no gem workspace and no interpreter (3433
# store paths, 0 Ruby, verified in CI). Most of the 65 ids in `.trivyignore` come
# from surfaces this variant does not contain. Scanning it against them cannot
# tell us its real CVE count in either direction — it can only hide findings.
#
# This variant is the one crossing into a FedRAMP High boundary, so it is the one
# that must be measured rather than asserted. An EMPTY file makes the scan report
# the truth: if the Ruby-free image is genuinely clean the gate passes on its own
# merits, and if it is not, we learn the real number instead of inheriting a
# number that was never about this artifact.
#
# The standing bar is real 0-CVE with an empty suppression surface — fix at
# cause, fork and patch upstream, never file an exception. Adding an id here
# would make this file exactly the thing it exists to replace.
